<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s06e03" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 3: Solving Equations Using Multiplication and Division</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 3 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Solve equations that look like <m>3x=9</m> or <m>\frac{x}{4}=2</m> using multiplication and division.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    In the previous episode, we saw that addition and subtraction were opposite actions. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          If we wanted to get rid of addition, we used subtraction. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If we wanted to get rid of subtraction, we used addition.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    Similarly, multiplication and division are opposite actions. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          If we want to get rid of multiplication, we use division.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If we want to get rid of division, we use multiplication.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    <alert>To Solve Equations of the Form [A Number]<m>\times</m> [Variable] = [Another Number]</alert>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Divide both sides of the equation by [A Number].
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve:
      <md>
        4x=20
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Divide both sides of the equation by 4:
      <md>
        \frac{4x}{4} = \frac{20}{4}
      </md>
      On the left, the multiplied 4 cancels out the divided 4. On the right, the 20 / 4 becomes 5. So the solution is
      <md>
        x=5
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve:
      <md>
        6x=15
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Divide both sides of the equation by 6:
      <md>
        \frac{6x}{6}=\frac{15}{6}
      </md>
      On the left side, the multiplied 6 and the divided 6 cancel out. On the right side, 15 / 6 can be simplified:
      <md>
        x=\frac{15}{6}
      </md>
      <md>
        x=\frac{15\div 3}{6\div 3}
      </md>
      <md>
        x=\frac{5}{2}
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve:
      <md>
        -2x=8
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Divide both sides of the equation by -2:
      <md>
        \frac{-2x}{-2} = \frac{8}{-2}
      </md>
      On the left side, the multiplied -2 cancels the divided -2. On the right side, the <m>8 \div (-2)</m> becomes -4.
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{3}x=\frac{1}{4}
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Divide both sides of the equation by 1/3:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{3}x\div\frac{1}{3} = \frac{1}{4}\div\frac{1}{3}
      </md>
      On the left side, the multiplied 1/3 and the divided 1/3 cancel out. On the right side, we perform fraction division:
      <md>
        x=\frac{1}{4}\div\frac{1}{3}
      </md>
      <md>
        x=\frac{1}{4}\times\frac{3}{1}
      </md>
      <md>
        x=\frac{1\times 3}{1\times 4}
      </md>
      <md>
        x=\frac{3}{4}
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    <alert>To Solve Equations of the Form [Variable]/[A Number] = [Another Number]</alert>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Multiply both sides of the equation by [A Number].
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve:
      <md>
        \frac{x}{3}=8
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Multiply both sides of the equation by 3:
      <md>
        \frac{x}{3}\times 3 = 8\times 3
      </md>
      On the left side, the divided 3 and the multiplied 3 cancel out. On the right side, the <m>8\times 3</m> becomes 24.
      <md>
        x=24
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve:
      <md>
        \frac{x}{7}=\frac{2}{5}
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Multiply both sides of the equation by 7:
      <md>
        \frac{x}{7}\times 7 = \frac{2}{5}\times 7
      </md>
      On the left side, the divided and the multiplied 7 cancel out. On the right side, we use fraction multiplication:
      <md>
        x=\frac{2}{5}\times 7
      </md>
      <md>
        x=\frac{2}{5}\times \frac{7}{1}
      </md>
      <md>
        x=\frac{2\times 7}{5\times 1}
      </md>
      <md>
        x=\frac{14}{5}
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Consider an expression 
    <md>
      -x
    </md>
    Remember from Season 2 that the - sign next to the <m>x</m> in this expression means “make it negative” or “calculate the opposite.” 
    <md>
    <mrow>-(3) \amp = -3 \,\,\,\, \text{(opposite of 3)}</mrow>
       <mrow>-(-2) \amp = 2 \,\,\,\, \text{(opposite of -2)}</mrow>
    </md>
    The expression <m>-x</m> is the same as
    <md>
      (-1)x
    </md>
    Because multiplying by -1 also has the effect of “opposite”:
    <md>
    <mrow>(-1)\times 3 \amp = -3 \,\,\,\, \text{(opposite of 3)}</mrow>
       <mrow>(-1)\times (-2) \amp = 2 \,\,\,\, \text{(opposite of -2)}</mrow>
    </md>
    We use this fact when solving equations that contain <m>-x</m>.
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve the equation:
      <md>
        -x=9
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Instead of <m>-x</m>, write <m>(-1)x</m>
      <md>
        (-1)x=9
      </md>
      Now we see we should divide both sides by -1:
      <md>
        \frac{(-1)x}{-1}=\frac{9}{-1}
      </md>
      On the left side, the multiplied -1 cancels out the divided -1, so we get
      <md>
        x=-9
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Again, equations can give us a way to take a tricky or complicated and break it down into simpler steps.
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Represent the price of a house by a variable, B. 
      You plan to make 25% of the house price as a down payment. If you only want to put $120,000 down, what house price can you afford?
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            The house price is being represented by the variable B. Let’s make an expression that represents the down payment, which is 25% of B:
            <md>
              0.25B
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            We want the down payment to be 120000. Let’s make an equation that represents this desire:
            <md>
              0.25B=120,000
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Now let’s solve this equation for B. 
            <md>
              \frac{0.25B}{0.25}=\frac{120,000}{0.25}
            </md>
            On the left side, the multiplied 0.25 and the divided 0.25 cancel, and we’re left with
            <md>
              B=480,000
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      So we’re looking for a house that costs around $480,000.
    </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s06e03">
    <title>Season 6, Episode 3 Practice Problems</title>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
      <p>
        Solve the following equations:
      </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>5x=40</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>0.8m=6.4</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y\div 6=7</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>n\div 0.5 = 12</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>-3a=18</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{2}{3}p=10</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{b}{4}=-5</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>q\div \frac{1}{3}=16</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>-7k=-56</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{5}{6}c=\frac{25}{6}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>d\div 2.5=4</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>1.5t=-9</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>-0.25f=5</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>w\div (-8) = 3</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{g}{-3}=\frac{7}{9}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>12h=3</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{4}{5}z=-8</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>j\div (-0.2)=-15</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.6in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>s\div \frac{3}{2}=10</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Represent the price of a house by a variable, B. You plan to make 20% of the house price as a down payment. 
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Write down an expression that represents the down payment.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Suppose you want to put $100,000 down. Write down an equation that represents this desire.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Solve the equation to determine the house price you can afford.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>
    
  </worksheet>
</section>  